 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np7-01",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "non coeptae adsurgunt turres, non arma iuventus",
   "trans": "the towers once begun do not rise, the young men do not",
   "line": "86",
   "body": "The repeated non lists everything that has stopped, so the city's paralysis is built up item by item.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-02",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "coeptae",
   "trans": "once begun",
   "line": "86",
   "body": "coeptae reminds the reader that Carthage was being built with energy in Book 1. Now the work has stopped, and the contrast shows the cost of Dido's love.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-03",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Personification",
   "quote": "pendent opera interrupta",
   "trans": "the work hangs interrupted",
   "line": "88",
   "body": "The unfinished buildings \"hang\" in mid-air, just as Dido \"hung\" on Aeneas' words in line 79. Her state of mind is reflected in her city.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "minaeque murorum ingentes",
   "trans": "the huge threatening walls",
   "line": "88–89",
   "body": "The walls are called \"threats\", but with no one building them they threaten nothing, an image of power left idle.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Hyperbole",
   "quote": "aequataque machina caelo",
   "trans": "the crane that reached the sky",
   "line": "89",
   "body": "The crane that once stood level with the sky now stands still, which makes the halt in work seem all the more striking.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-06",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Metaphor",
   "quote": "tali persensit peste teneri",
   "trans": "saw that she was held by such a plague",
   "line": "90",
   "body": "Dido's love is called a pestis, a disease or ruin, which presents it as something destructive and contagious.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-07",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "nec famam obstare furori",
   "trans": "and that her reputation did not stand in the way of her madness",
   "line": "91",
   "body": "Dido's good name no longer holds her back. fama, reputation, will become important later in the book.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-08",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "cara Iovis coniunx",
   "trans": "Jupiter's dear wife",
   "line": "91",
   "body": "Juno is called \"dear\" to Jupiter, an ironic title for a goddess whose marriage is famously quarrelsome, and who is about to scheme behind his back.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-09",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Epithet",
   "quote": "Saturnia",
   "trans": "daughter of Saturn",
   "line": "92",
   "body": "Saturnia recalls Juno's descent from Saturn, an older and harsher generation of gods, which hints at her stubborn anger.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-10",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Sarcasm",
   "quote": "egregiam vero laudem et spolia ampla refertis",
   "trans": "truly outstanding glory and rich spoils you are winning",
   "line": "93",
   "body": "Juno uses the language of military triumph mockingly: Venus and Cupid have done nothing more heroic than defeat one woman.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-11",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Parenthesis",
   "quote": "magnum et memorabile numen",
   "trans": "a great and memorable divine power",
   "line": "94",
   "body": "The aside is heavily sarcastic. The grand language mocks Venus and Cupid for ganging up on a mortal.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-12",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Juxtaposition",
   "quote": "una dolo divum si femina victa duorum est",
   "trans": "if one woman has been conquered by the trickery of two gods",
   "line": "95",
   "body": "una ... duorum frames the line, setting one woman against two gods. Juno makes the \"victory\" sound as shameful as possible.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-13",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Rhythm",
   "quote": "una dolo divum",
   "trans": "one … by the trick of the gods",
   "line": "95",
   "body": "The heavy d sounds and the pointed placement of dolo stress the deceit behind Dido's love.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-14",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "nec me adeo fallit",
   "trans": "nor does it escape me",
   "line": "96",
   "body": "Juno shows she understands Venus' fears, which makes her seem shrewd and sets up her offer as reasonable.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-15",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Possessive",
   "quote": "moenia nostra",
   "trans": "our walls",
   "line": "96",
   "body": "Juno calls Carthage's walls \"ours\", reminding Venus that Carthage is her city.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-16",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical question",
   "quote": "sed quis erit modus, aut quo nunc certamine tanto?",
   "trans": "but what will be the limit, or where is this great contest leading now?",
   "line": "98",
   "body": "Juno ends with questions that make her seem weary of the conflict and ready to compromise, which prepares for her offer in the next lines.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Cupid",
   "quote": "puerque tuus",
   "trans": "and that boy of yours",
   "line": "94",
   "body": "Venus' son Cupid, who in Book 1 took the form of Ascanius and breathed love into Dido when she embraced him.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 7,
 "questions": [
  "Lines 86–89 describe the building work coming to a halt. Why does Virgil include this here, and what does it say about Dido as a ruler?",
  "How does Juno's speech (93–98) combine flattery and sarcasm? What does she really want from Venus?",
  "Virgil calls Juno \"cara Iovis coniunx\" and \"Saturnia\" in the same breath. What do these names suggest about her power and her character?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Juno and Venus",
   "body": "<p>The two goddesses are old enemies. Juno, wife of Jupiter, hates the Trojans: Paris judged Venus more beautiful than her, and she knows that Aeneas' descendants are fated to destroy her favourite city, Carthage. Venus is Aeneas' mother and protects him at every turn; in Book 1 she sent Cupid, disguised as Ascanius, to make Dido fall in love with him.</p><p>Here Juno tries a new tactic. If she cannot stop Aeneas reaching Italy, perhaps she can keep him in Carthage by marrying him to Dido. Venus sees through the plan but goes along with it, because it keeps her son safe for now.</p>"
  }
 ]
